# ---------------- Petra ----------------
meta={
"type":"place","title_zh":"佩特拉：峡谷里的商队首都","title_en":"Petra: A Caravan Capital in the Canyon",
"era":"约公元前 4 世纪–公元 4 世纪","era_en":"c. 4th century BCE–4th century CE",
"region":"约旦，马安省，瓦迪穆萨","region_en":"Wadi Musa, Ma'an Governorate, Jordan",
"coords":[30.3285,35.4444],"tags":["佩特拉","纳巴泰","岩凿建筑","约旦","商队"],"collections":[],"credibility":"verified",
"related":["jiaohe","troy"],
"en_headings":["Through the Siq","Water matters more than stone","Rock-cut façades and tombs","Caravans, incense, and Rome","Earthquake, abandonment, and 'rediscovery'","Conservation and tourism pressure"],
"limits_zh":"佩特拉的许多建筑没有铭文，建造年代只能靠风格、结构关系和少量文字推断。纳巴泰人的宗教仪式、社会分层和日常生活在文献中留下的痕迹很少，更多依赖遗址本身与邻近地区的比较。游客最熟悉的卡兹尼神殿，其功能仍有多种解释；把“宝库”当成确定名称，是旅游叙事留下的误会。本文把可测的建筑、可读的铭文与后来的传说分层处理。",
"limits_en":"Many buildings at Petra carry no inscriptions, so their dates must be inferred from style, structural relationships, and a few texts. The Nabataeans' religious practice, social stratification, and daily life left few traces in written sources, and depend more on the site itself and comparisons with neighbouring regions. The function of the Treasury, the monument visitors know best, remains disputed; treating 'Treasury' as a settled name is a misunderstanding left by tourism. This entry layers measurable architecture, readable inscriptions, and later legend separately.",
}
sections=[
("一、穿过蛇道","进入佩特拉，通常要先穿过一条狭窄的峡谷，阿拉伯语叫“西克”。两侧岩壁高几十米，底部只有几米宽，阳光只在正午短暂照入。峡谷尽头，卡兹尼神殿的立面突然出现在岩壁上。这个入口不是装饰，而是城市防御与水利系统的一部分：狭窄的通道可以控制人流，岩壁上的水槽和陶管则把山洪引向蓄水池。佩特拉不是一座“藏在山谷里的秘密城市”，而是一座把峡谷本身当作基础设施的都城。","Entering Petra usually means walking through a narrow gorge called the Siq. Its walls rise tens of metres, the floor is only a few metres wide, and sunlight reaches the bottom only briefly around noon. At the end, the façade of al-Khazneh appears suddenly on the rock wall. The entrance is not decoration but part of the city's defence and water system: the narrow passage controls movement, while channels and terracotta pipes on the walls direct flash floods into cisterns. Petra was not a 'secret city hidden in a valley' but a capital that used the gorge itself as infrastructure."),
("二、水比石头更关键","佩特拉年降雨量很低，而且常以短时暴雨出现。纳巴泰人修建了蓄水池、引水槽、水坝和地下管道，把雨水收集起来，分配到城市和农田。遗址里能看到渠道沿着岩壁延伸，也能看到被洪水冲毁后重修的痕迹。没有这套水利系统，这座在峡谷中扩张的城市无法维持人口。理解佩特拉，不能只看立面，还要看水从哪里来、往哪里去、由谁维护。","Petra receives little annual rain, and what falls often comes in short, violent storms. The Nabataeans built cisterns, channels, dams, and underground conduits to collect and distribute water to the city and its fields. Traces of channels running along the cliffs are still visible, as are repairs after flood damage. Without this water system, the city could not have sustained its population in the gorge. To understand Petra, one must look not only at façades but at where water came from, where it went, and who maintained it."),
("三、岩凿的立面与墓葬","佩特拉最著名的建筑大多不是独立建造的，而是从砂岩崖壁上凿出来的。卡兹尼神殿、修道院、罗马式剧场和大量墓葬立面，共享一套“先凿出平面、再向下雕刻”的工艺。立面融合了纳巴泰、希腊化、埃及和罗马的元素，说明这里长期处在贸易与文化交流的交叉点。很多立面其实是墓葬，后面连着墓室；游客看到的“城市立面”，有一部分是死者世界的门面。","Most of Petra's famous buildings were not built up from the ground but carved out of sandstone cliffs. al-Khazneh, the Monastery, the Roman theatre, and many tomb façades share a technique of cutting a flat face first and then carving inward. The façades combine Nabataean, Hellenistic, Egyptian, and Roman elements, showing that the city stood at a crossing of trade and cultural exchange. Many façades are actually tombs with chambers behind them; part of the 'city front' visitors see is the front door of the dead."),
("四、商队、香料与罗马","佩特拉位于从阿拉伯半岛向北通往地中海东岸的商路上，乳香、没药、香料和布匹经过这里。纳巴泰人从游牧群体发展为控制贸易节点的王国，公元前 4 世纪以后逐渐强盛。公元 106 年，罗马帝国吞并纳巴泰王国，佩特拉继续作为区域城市存在，但贸易路线和权力结构发生变化。它的繁荣不是一条商路单独带来的，而是水源、地理位置、政治联盟和税收共同作用的结果。","Petra stood on the caravan routes from the Arabian Peninsula to the eastern Mediterranean, and frankincense, myrrh, spices, and textiles passed through it. The Nabataeans developed from a nomadic group into a kingdom controlling trade nodes and grew stronger from the fourth century BCE onward. In 106 CE the Roman Empire annexed the Nabataean kingdom; Petra continued as a regional city, but trade routes and power structures changed. Its prosperity was not produced by a single road but by water, location, political alliances, and taxation together."),
("五、地震、废弃与“重新发现”","公元 363 年的一次大地震损坏了佩特拉的大量建筑，此后城市逐渐衰落。贸易路线转移、政治中心的变动和水利系统的失修，使它在伊斯兰早期以后不再是主要城市。1812 年，瑞士探险家约翰·路德维希·布克哈特在瓦迪穆萨听到当地人提到峡谷中的遗址，随后进入佩特拉；欧洲旅行者把这次访问写成“重新发现”。但当地贝都因人一直知道并居住在这里，所谓“发现”是外部视角的叙事。","A major earthquake in 363 CE damaged much of Petra, and the city declined thereafter. Shifting trade routes, changes in political centres, and the decay of the water system meant that it was no longer a major city after the early Islamic period. In 1812 the Swiss explorer Johann Ludwig Burckhardt heard locals in Wadi Musa mention the site in the gorge and then entered Petra; European travellers described the visit as a 'rediscovery'. But local Bedouin communities had always known and lived there, and the 'discovery' is an outside narrative."),
("六、保护与旅游的压力","佩特拉在 1985 年列入世界遗产。今天它面对的是岩体风化、盐蚀、洪水、地震风险和大量游客。遗址内的步道、台阶和墓室被反复踩踏，部分区域不得不限制进入。当地社区与遗址管理方的关系也很复杂：旅游收入、迁居、放牧权和遗产保护经常冲突。保护不是把遗址封起来，而是要在岩体稳定、洪水管理和社区生计之间找到可持续的平衡。","Petra was inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 1985. Today it faces rock weathering, salt erosion, flash floods, earthquake risk, and heavy visitation. Paths, steps, and tomb chambers are repeatedly trampled, and some areas have had to restrict access. Relations between local communities and site managers are also complex: tourism income, relocation, grazing rights, and heritage protection often conflict. Conservation is not a matter of sealing the site off but of finding a sustainable balance between rock stability, flood management, and local livelihoods."),
]
